<h1>NAT, Port Forwarding, and Why "Send Me Your IP" Isn't Enough</h1>
<p>Hosting works instantly on your LAN and mysteriously not for a friend across
town. The reason is the router in your closet.</p>

<h2>Private vs public addresses</h2>
<p>Your machine's address (from <code>ifconfig</code>/<code>ipconfig</code>) is almost certainly
something like <code>192.168.1.20</code> — a <strong>private</strong> address. Whole ranges
(<code>10.x.x.x</code>, <code>172.16-31.x.x</code>, <code>192.168.x.x</code>) are reserved for
home/office LANs and are <em>not routable</em> on the internet; millions of homes reuse
the same numbers. Your household shares <em>one</em> public IP, held by the router.</p>

<h2>NAT: the reason outbound works and inbound doesn't</h2>
<p><strong>Network Address Translation</strong> is the router rewriting your traffic: when you
connect out, it swaps your private source address for its public one and remembers
the mapping so replies find their way back. That memory is keyed by <em>connections
you initiated</em>. An unsolicited inbound connection — say, a friend's game client
dialing your public IP on port 40605 — matches no mapping, so the router drops it.
That asymmetry is why browsing works everywhere but <em>hosting</em> needs setup.</p>

<h2>Three ways in</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Approach</th><th>How</th><th>Trade-off</th></tr>
<tr><td>Port forwarding</td><td>Router config: "TCP 40605 → 192.168.1.20". Friends use your <em>public</em> IP.</td><td>Free, standard; needs router admin access, exposes the port to the whole internet</td></tr>
<tr><td>Overlay VPN (Tailscale, ZeroTier)</td><td>Every machine joins a private virtual network and gets a stable address (e.g. <code>100.x.y.z</code>); friends join your "tailnet" and use that</td><td>Zero router config, encrypted, invite-only; everyone installs an app</td></tr>
<tr><td>Relay / NAT punching</td><td>A third-party server brokers or relays the connection (what commercial games do)</td><td>Someone must run that infrastructure — out of scope for v1</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="callout good">For "me and three friends", the overlay VPN is usually the best
answer: no router menus, no open ports, and the game needs zero code changes —
the host's Tailscale IP goes in the same join box as a LAN address.</div>

<h2>Why the game itself stays out of it</h2>
<p>NAT traversal (STUN/TURN/ICE, UPnP) is a deep well of protocol work that mostly
exists so <em>strangers</em> can connect without configuring anything. This game's v1
scope is friends who can coordinate ("install Tailscale" fits in one text
message), so the engineering budget went into the game instead. The README says
so explicitly — documenting a limitation honestly beats half-implementing a
solution.</p>

<h2>Debugging "can't connect" in this game</h2>
<ol>
<li>Same machine? <code>127.0.0.1:40605</code> must work — if not, the host isn't up
(check the Multiplayer page status line).</li>
<li>Same LAN? Use the host's <code>192.168.x.x</code>. Failure here is usually the OS
firewall prompting the host to allow the app, or club Wi-Fi isolating clients.</li>
<li>Across the internet? You need one of the three approaches above — a plain
public IP will time out, and the game will say so via its 4-second connect
timeout rather than hanging.</li>
</ol>
